  "page_placements": [],
  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "None noted; the full paper text is present.",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper discusses language-model scaling laws and reports RLVR fine-tuning results, but it does not measure pretraining loss, fit the claimed power laws, test extrapolation from smaller pretraining runs, or find a compute-optimal parameter/token allocation.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports post-fine-tuning test accuracy across training-set sizes and compositions. These are related results on data scaling in RLVR, but they are not measurements of the loss laws or other behaviors named by the claim versions. No claim-specific result rows are included; the accuracy results are recorded as related findings under fits_no_version.",
    "test_reason": "The study cites Kaplan and Hoffmann only in related work. Its own results are post-RLVR task accuracy for one 4B model across 100–500 training examples under a fixed step budget. It measures no pretraining or held-out loss, fits no power law, varies no parameters or compute, and tests no extrapolation. Its non-monotonic data-size trends come from LoRA RLVR with fixed compute, a different construct from any version, so they go under fits_no_version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. Add that the authors also report that models RLVR-trained on easy tasks generalise to harder test tasks. They describe mixed-difficulty data as giving up to 5x sample efficiency. They hypothesise an inverted-U data-scaling curve under fixed compute that would contrast with monotonic SFT scaling laws, but present it as preliminary and not a universal law.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study performs LoRA-based RLVR fine-tuning of one 4B model while varying small training datasets and composition, then measures task accuracy. It does not reproduce pretraining-loss scaling experiments or estimate parameter/token compute-optimal allocations.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported outcomes vary by task domain and training-set composition. The authors also identify fixed training duration, token limits, and incomplete graph-task rollouts as constraints.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Under fixed-step LoRA RLVR on Qwen3-4B, test accuracy is non-monotonic in training-set size in several settings. Counting Mixed falls from 44.2% to 35.5%, and Spatial Easy peaks at 200 examples. 100 mixed-difficulty examples match 500 easy ones on Counting (44.2% each).",
        "proposed_claim": "In low-data, fixed-compute RLVR fine-tuning, post-training accuracy does not improve reliably with more examples; data composition can outweigh quantity.",
        "row_ids": [
          "R01",
          "R02",
